    "introduction": "August 2026 marks a milestone in Vietnam–China mobility with Vietnam's inclusion under China's 240-hour (10-day) Visa-Free Transit regime—unlocking unprecedented flexibility for short-term cultural travel, market reconnaissance, and business engagements.",
    "historicalContext": "China's Transit Without Visa (TWOV) liberalization expanded progressively from 72 hours (2013) to 144 hours (2016) before being uniformly upgraded by the National Immigration Administration (NIA) to **240 hours (10 days)** across 60+ ports in December 2024, followed by inter-provincial zone updates in 2025–2026. Immigration specialists nonetheless stress two invariant rules of TWOV governance: (1) nationality eligibility is strictly defined by periodic official NIA bulletins—Vietnamese citizens must verify prior to departure which ports accept ordinary Vietnamese passports or whether a standard visa or ASEAN tour-group waiver is required; and (2) the 240-hour TWOV policy is legally distinct from Guilin's '144-Hour ASEAN Tour-Group Visa-Free Policy,' which applies exclusively to organized groups from the ten ASEAN nations handled by accredited travel agencies.",
