    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Between the 14th and 16th centuries, Vietnam and China engaged in a remarkable artistic and commercial rivalry along the East Sea Maritime Porcelain Road. During the Ming court's 15th-century Haijin (海禁) ban on private overseas ceramic exports, kilns in Đại Việt—spearheaded by Chu Đậu (Hải Dương) along with Thăng Long and Lái Thiêu—stepped swiftly into the global vacuum, supplying Japan (where Vietnamese ceramics were prized in tea ceremonies as Kōchi or Annan-yaki), Southeast Asia (evidenced by the Cù Lao Chàm shipwreck carrying over 240,000 Chu Đậu artifacts), and even the Ottoman Topkapi Sarayi Palace in Istanbul, which preserves a 54-centimeter Chu Đậu blue-and-white bottle signed by female master potter Bùi Thị Hý in 1450. While sharing underglaze cobalt oxide chemistry with Jingdezhen, Chu Đậu ceramics exhibit unmistakably freer, lyric brushwork rooted in Red River Delta nature—darting warblers, scrolling chrysanthemums, butterflies, and flute-playing buffalo boys—paired with warm ivory bodies, multi-tiered lotus-petal bands, and a Lý–Trần celadon lotus-bowl tradition worthy of Song stoneware.",
