    "theoreticalFramework": "Classical Chinese literary theory revolves around two central conceptual dyads: (1) 'Word–Meaning–Image' (言–意–象), stretching from the Book of Changes to Liu Xie, whereby finite language deploys concrete imagery (Xiàng) to evoke meanings that transcend explicit phrasing ('meaning residing beyond words', 意在言外); and (2) Confucius's ideal of 'refinement and substance in balanced harmony' (文质彬彬), insisting on equilibrium between aesthetic form (Wén) and ethical or emotional substance (Zhì). Methodologically, scholars reading classical texts must disentangle three analytical strata: textual transmission and variant recensions (such as distinguishing Cao Xueqin's original 80 chapters of Dream of the Red Chamber from the latter 40 chapters), intertextual classical allusions, and urban vernacular registers.",
