    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Premodern and early modern Vietnamese literature conducted one of East Asia's deepest creative dialogues with Chinese literary tradition. First, Đại Việt literati mastered Tang-style regulated verse (seven-character octaves and quatrains) in Literary Chinese (Lý Thường Kiệt, Nguyễn Trãi, Nguyễn Du), while indigenizing those very forms into vernacular Nôm regulated verse (Hồ Xuân Hương, Bà Huyện Thanh Quan) infused with flexible prosody and incisive Vietnamese idiom. Second, Chinese imperial prose genres—edicts, memorials, proclamations, and indictments (Chiếu, Biểu, Hịch, Cáo)—became instruments of sovereign self-assertion in Vietnam, exemplified by Nguyễn Trãi's *Great Proclamation upon the Pacification of the Wu* (*Bình Ngô đại cáo*), cast in impeccable *tứ lục* parallel prose while positing the Triệu–Đinh–Lý–Trần dynasties as sovereign peers of the Han, Tang, Song, and Yuan. Third, rather than mechanically translating Ming–Qing prose novels, the poet Nguyễn Du transformed Qingxin Cairen's 20-chapter prose romance *Jin Yun Qiao Zhuan* into the transcendent vernacular *lục bát* (6–8 syllable) verse epic *The Tale of Kiều* (*Truyện Kiều*), achieving psychological lyricism far surpassing its prose source; similarly, Đoàn Thị Điểm rendered Đặng Trần Côn's *Lament of the Soldier's Wife* (*Chinh phụ ngâm*) into haunting *song thất lục bát* verse. In the twentieth century, the May Fourth Movement and Lu Xun likewise shaped Vietnamese critical realism and narrative theory between 1930 and 1945.",
