    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Vietnam stands as one of the rare nations in Southeast Asia to share deeply in the 'Unity of the Three Teachings' (Tam giáo đồng nguyên)—reaching its zenith during the Lý–Trần dynasties, epitomized by the Trúc Lâm Yên Tử Zen school founded by Emperor-Monk Trần Nhân Tông, which fused engaged Confucian statecraft with sudden Chan awakening—while grounding it upon a fiercely autonomous Vietnamese spiritual bedrock. In contrast to northern Chinese patrilineal ritual emphasis, Vietnamese consciousness preserves strong Southeast Asian matrifocal currents through the Mother Goddesses of the Three/Four Realms (Đạo Mẫu Tam phủ – Tứ phủ, UNESCO ICH 2016), nationwide veneration of the Hùng Kings as shared national ancestors (elevating lineage worship to the scale of the entire nation on the 10th day of the 3rd lunar month), and Village Tutelary Deity (Thành Hoàng) worship in communal Đình halls. When Minh Hương immigrants settled in Hội An, Phố Hiến, and Chợ Lớn during the 17th–18th centuries, Fujian, Chaozhou, and Cantonese assembly halls (Tianhou and Lord Guan temples) integrated peacefully into southern Vietnamese religious life, demonstrating the non-conflictual hospitality of both spiritual cultures.",
