    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Traditional Vietnamese dress (Việt phục) and Hanfu stand in a relationship of 'shared structural roots, refined localization, and independent aesthetic line.' From the Lý, Trần, and Early Lê dynasties, the court of Đại Việt adopted the right-overlapping cross-collar (Áo Giao lĩnh) and round-collar official robe (Áo Viên lĩnh)—illustrated in portraits of Nguyễn Trãi wearing a winged phốc đầu cap and round-collar court robe aligned with broader East Asian ceremonial norms. Yet Vietnamese artisans dramatically adapted garments for a humid monsoon climate, favoring breathable Vạn Phúc silk, glossy black Lãnh Mỹ A silk, and earthy brown or indigo dyes in vernacular attire such as the iconic four-panel Áo Tứ thân knotted over a diamond-shaped yếm đào bodice. Following Lord Nguyễn Phúc Khoát's 1744 Đàng Trong dress reform and the Nguyễn dynasty (19th century), Vietnam perfected the five-panel Áo Dài Ngũ thân—fastened under the right arm beneath a standing collar (lập lĩnh)—and the royal Áo Nhật bình, whose symmetrical rectangular collar engaged Ming phoenix-robe motifs while displaying distinctly Huế court waves, dragons, and phoenixes. Today, both Vietnam's five-panel Áo dài revival and China's New Chinese Style / Mamianqun movement reflect parallel 21st-century renaissances of national sartorial heritage.",
