    "introduction": "Many Vietnamese enterprises falter in the very first meeting because they apply an identical communication playbook to counterparties in Beijing, Shanghai, and Shenzhen. Mastering China's regional geo-commercial matrix is essential to reading a partner's incentives and decision-making cadence.",
    "historicalContext": "The divergence in Chinese regional merchant cultures is no modern accident; it stems from historical regional merchant guilds (商帮 Shāngbāng) during the Ming and Qing dynasties. In the north, Shanxi merchants (晋商) and capital merchants (京商) grew through salt-and-iron monopolies, Pingyao remittance draft banks, and imperial patronage in Beijing. Along the Yangtze delta, Huizhou merchants (徽商) and Zhejiang merchants (浙商—Ningbo, Wenzhou, Hangzhou) matured through silk, tea, and publishing networks feeding into Shanghai's 19th–20th-century chambers of commerce. Meanwhile, Cantonese and Chaoshan merchants (粤商) were forged in maritime foreign trade through Canton's Thirteen Hongs (十三行) and Southeast Asian overseas Chinese networks.",
