    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Vietnam absorbed these four technologies remarkably early and localized them using tropical forest resources. By the 3rd century CE, Chinese dynastic sources record Jiaozhou (Giao Châu) producing exquisite 'honey-fragrance paper' (Mật hương chỉ, made from agarwood bark) presented as tribute to the Jin court; in later centuries, traditional Giấy Dó (poonah paper) craft in Bưởi village (Yên Thái, Hanoi) reached pinnacle durability, preserving Đông Hồ folk woodblock prints and royal decrees free from insect decay for centuries. In printing, 15th-century envoy Lương Nhữ Hộc brought advanced woodblock-carving techniques back to establish the Liễu Tràng and Hồng Lục printing guilds (Hải Dương), enabling Đại Việt to carve the complete 1697 edition of the Complete Annals of Đại Việt (Đại Việt Sử Ký Toàn Thư), while Hồ Nguyên Trừng advanced cannon-casting technology in early 15th-century East Asia.",
