    "abstract": "A geological and cultural-historical anatomy of the Guilin–Yangshuo–Longji heritage triangle in Guangxi: from 300 million years of Devonian–Carboniferous limestone dissolution creating the signature 'Peak Forest and Peak Cluster' (峰林 Fēnglín – 峰丛 Fēngcóng) tower karst depicted on China's 20-yuan banknote, to the Ming-era Jingjiang Princely City (1372) enclosing Solitary Beauty Peak and the Southern Song inscription 'Guilin's mountains and waters are finest under Heaven' (桂林山水甲天下) by Wang Zhenggong, alongside the millennia-old terraced ecosystems of the Zhuang (壮族) and Yao (瑶族) peoples.",
    "keyTakeaways": [
      "The tower-karst topography of Guilin and Yangshuo is inscribed as a UNESCO World Natural Heritage site (part of the South China Karst serial property).",
      "Built in early Ming (1372) around Solitary Beauty Peak, the Jingjiang Princely City (靖江王城) is among the best-preserved Ming princely palace complexes in China.",
      "The Longji Rice Terraces (龙脊梯田) have been continuously sculpted and irrigated by Zhuang and Red Yao communities for more than 650 years since the Yuan and Ming dynasties."
    ],
    "introduction": "The celebrated saying 'Guilin's mountains and waters are finest under Heaven; Yangshuo's mountains and waters surpass even Guilin's' (桂林山水甲天下，阳朔山水甲桂林) extols both ink-wash natural scenery and an extraordinary historical contact zone between Huaxia civilization—exemplified by the Qin-dynasty Lingqu Canal—and ancient Baiyue peoples ancestral to the Zhuang and Yao.",
    "historicalContext": "In 1201 during the Southern Song dynasty, Wang Zhenggong (王正功 Wáng Zhènggōng), Judicial Intendant of Guangnan West Circuit, composed the cliff-carved poem on Solitary Beauty Peak bearing the immortal line 'Guilin's landscapes are finest under Heaven' (桂林山水甲天下)—the earliest textual anchor for the region's renown (while the pairing 'Yangshuo's landscapes surpass Guilin's' emerged in later lore). Centuries earlier, in 214 BCE under Qin Shi Huang, engineer Shi Lu (史禄 Shǐ Lù) excavated the Lingqu Canal (灵渠 Língqú) in Xing'an near Guilin to link the Xiang River (Yangtze basin) with the Li River (Pearl River basin), creating the hydraulic artery that carried Central Plains civilization southward into Lingnan and Jiaozhi.",
    "mainContent": [
      {
        "heading": "1. Li River Karst Geomorphology & the 20-Yuan Banknote Vista",
        "body": "Sailing roughly 83 km downstream from Zhujiang Pier (竹江码头) or Mopanshan Pier (磨盘山码头) to Yangshuo, riverboats pass Nine-Horse Fresco Hill (九马画山) and Yellow Cloth Shoal Reflection (黄布倒影) at Xingping—the exact panorama engraved on the reverse of the RMB 20 banknote."
      },
      {
        "heading": "2. Jingjiang Princely Palace (靖江王府) & Guangxi's Literati Heritage",
        "body": "Constructed in 1372 for Zhu Shouqian (grandnephew of Ming founder Zhu Yuanzhang), Jingjiang Princely City encloses a 1,560-meter perimeter around Solitary Beauty Peak (独秀峰), where Wang Zhenggong's Song-dynasty poem remains incised in stone."
      },
      {
        "heading": "3. Longji Terraces & Zhuang–Yao Cultural Traditions",
        "body": "Roughly two hours' drive north of Guilin in Longsheng County, the Ping'an Zhuang Terraces (平安寨) and Jinkeng Red Yao Terraces (金坑大寨) climb from 300 to 1,100 meters in elevation. Highlights include the long-hair heritage of Red Yao women in Huangluo Village (黄洛瑶寨) and fragrant bamboo-tube rice (竹筒饭)."
      },
      {
        "heading": "4. Decoding the 20-Yuan Li River Panorama & the Qin-Era Lingqu Canal",
        "body": "The vignette on the fifth series RMB 20 note (issued from 1999) replicates the view at Yellow Cloth Shoal Reflection (黄布倒影) in Xingping Town (兴坪 Xīngpíng), Yangshuo, where Yuanbao and Luohan limestone peaks mirror in calm Li River waters. Just to the north, the 214 BCE Lingqu Canal deploys a V-shaped spillway dam known as the 'Plowshare' (铧嘴) to split river flow in a 7:3 ratio between the Xiang and Li rivers, aided by 36 flash-lock gates representing one of the earliest canal-lock systems in human hydraulic history."
      },
      {
        "heading": "5. Zhuang & Red Yao Heritage at Longji & Seasonal Irrigation Cycles",
        "body": "Within the Longji Scenic Area (80 km from Guilin), Ping'an Zhuang Village, Jinkeng Dazhai (Red Yao), and Ancient Zhuang Village preserve three-story moisture-proof wooden stilt architecture alongside Red Yao women's long-hair custom (often exceeding 1.4 meters as an emblem of longevity and vitality). Across four seasons, the terraces transform dramatically: flooded mirrors reflecting the sky in May–June, lush emerald shoots in July–August, golden harvest waves in September–October, and crystalline frost or snow from December through February."
      }
    ],
    "theoreticalFramework": "Guilin serves in international geomorphology as the global type locality for humid tropical Tower Karst—comprising isolated limestone towers (Peak Forest / 峰林 Fēnglín) and coalesced cones (Peak Cluster / 峰丛 Fēngcóng)—sculpted when tectonic uplift alternated with carbonic-acid rainwater dissolution across thousands of meters of Devonian-to-Carboniferous marine carbonate strata. In ecological anthropology, the Longji Terraces stand as a FAO Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System (GIAHS), demonstrating indigenous gravity-fed hydrology channeling water from upland bamboo-and-pine headwater forests down thousands of contour-hugging paddies.",
