    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "Vietnam assimilated the legacy of the Hundred Schools in a pragmatic yet profound manner. Rather than mimicking harsh Shang Yang–style Legalism, the Lý, Trần, and Lê dynasties cultivated a synthesis of statecraft Confucianism (exemplified by Lê Thánh Tông and Chu Văn An), transparent rule-by-law governance (the Hình thư [Book of Punishments] and the Hồng Đức-era Quốc triều Hình luật [Penal Code of the National Dynasty], which protected women's rights and communal lands), and a humane Daoist–Zen ethos of leniency (captured in Trần Hưng Đạo's counsel to 'spare and nurture the people's strength'). Even in the 20th century, Vietnamese intellectuals such as Phan Bội Châu, Đào Duy Anh, and Nguyễn Hiến Lê retranslated and reinterpreted the Hundred Schools to engage directly with modern Western philosophy.",
