    "introduction": "Often pictured simplistically as a single desert camel trail, the historical 'Silk Road' actually operated as a complementary dual-axis network: a chain of Central Asian oases during the Han–Tang era, paired with an East Asian monsoon maritime highway during the Song–Yuan–Ming periods, in which the Shibosi maritime customs offices and the trading ports of Dai Viet served as indispensable links.",
    "historicalContext": "In the 2nd century BCE, Emperor Wu of Han dispatched Zhang Qian across the Taklamakan Desert to establish diplomatic ties with Dayuan, the Greater Yuezhi, and Anxi (Persia), inaugurating the formal Overland Silk Road linking Chang'an with the Roman Empire (Daqin). Over the Tang–Song–Yuan period, as steppe and desert warfare disrupted overland corridors and Fujian shipbuilders perfected the magnetic compass, axial sternpost rudder, and watertight multi-bulkhead hull design, the center of commercial gravity shifted decisively toward the sea—anchored by the Port of Quanzhou ('Zaytun' in Marco Polo's travels, designated a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2021). In the 15th century (1405–1433), Admiral Zheng He's colossal armada mounted seven expeditions to the 'Western Oceans' (Xia Xiyang) with nearly 28,000 crewmen before the Ming court curtailed official maritime outreach and retreated into its Haijin (maritime ban) policy.",
