    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "In legal and commercial translation between China and Vietnam, two notorious traps recur constantly: (1) The term 'shǒuduàn' (手段): in Chinese contracts or project proposals it routinely denotes a neutral 'technical measure or operational method,' whereas translating it mechanically into Vietnamese 'thủ đoạn' implies malicious cunning; (2) 'Xiéyì' (协议, a legally binding agreement) vs. 'Hétóng' (合同, a full formal contract), and 'fāngbiàn' (方便, meaning 'convenient' or 'restroom,' never 'means of transport'). Understanding the history of Sino-Vietnamese semantic shift prevents diplomatic misunderstandings and costly commercial disputes.",
    "examples": [
      "Real-world negotiation scenario: When a Chinese partner says '我们遇到了一点困难，需要做个检讨', the accurate meaning is 'We encountered a minor difficulty and need to conduct an internal self-review to learn lessons'—never to be misread through colloquial Vietnamese false-friend associations.",
      "Telex Release (`电放提单`) shipping scenario: Ocean transit from Shanghai to Hải Phòng takes only 4–5 days (faster than couriering original paper documents), so parties routinely agree that upon receipt of the 70% T/T balance, the seller executes `电放` (Surrendered B/L) allowing immediate cargo release without original paper bills."
    ],
