    "sinoVietnameseComparison": "The Tang era left a complex historical imprint on Vietnam, then administered from 679 as the Protectorate General to Pacify the South (Annam Đô Hộ Phủ). On the one hand, it witnessed resolute independence uprisings led by Mai Thúc Loan (722) and Phùng Hưng (8th century). On the other hand, through direct maritime Buddhist pilgrimages and scholarly exchange via Jiaozhou (including Tang-era Zen master Đại Thừa Đăng), Vietnamese elites deeply assimilated Chan (Thiền) Buddhism and Tang regulated prosody—exemplified by Khương Công Phụ (731–805) of Ái Châu (Thanh Hóa), who placed first in the decree examination at Chang'an and served as Grand Councilor (Prime Minister) under Emperor Dezong—before forging the independent Lý and Trần dynasties after 938.",
